{% extends 'dash/help/metric/layout.html.twig' %}

{% block definition %}
    <p>This card shows key PHP-FPM settings and runtime information for the current pool.</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block why_it_matters %}
    <p>These settings control how PHP processes are managed. They directly impact performance, resource usage, and how your server handles traffic.</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block how_to_read %}
    <ul class="d-flex flex-column gap-2">
        <li>
            <twig:Html:Badge:Neutral class="fs-6">pool</twig:Html:Badge:Neutral>
            <p>Name of the PHP-FPM pool. A pool is a group of worker processes with its own configuration.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <twig:Html:Badge:Neutral class="fs-6">mode (process manager)</twig:Html:Badge:Neutral>
            <p>Defines how PHP-FPM manages worker processes.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><twig:Html:Badge:Neutral>static</twig:Html:Badge:Neutral> A fixed number of workers is always running. Predictable performance, but can waste resources if traffic is low.</li>
                <li><twig:Html:Badge:Neutral>dynamic</twig:Html:Badge:Neutral> The number of workers adapts to the load. Starts with a few workers and creates more when needed.</li>
                <li><twig:Html:Badge:Neutral>ondemand</twig:Html:Badge:Neutral> Workers are created only when requests come in and stopped when idle. Saves resources but can add slight delay on first request.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li>
            <twig:Html:Badge:Neutral class="fs-6">uptime</twig:Html:Badge:Neutral>
            <p>Time since the PHP-FPM service started. Helps understand how long the current state has been running.</p>
        </li>
    </ul>
{% endblock %}
